   "",
   "in class, by teacher",
   "1. Correct Code Execution (50%)\n\nCode runs without errors and produces the expected results.\n\nExample:\n\nHomography matrix is computed correctly and image is warped properly.\n\nEdge detection code outputs clear edges (e.g., using Canny or Sobel).\n\nObject detection code detects the correct number of objects in a test image.\n\n2. Conceptual Understanding (20%)\n\nThe learner demonstrates understanding of the underlying theory.\n\nExamples:\n\nCan explain what a homography is (a transformation mapping points between two planes).\n\nUnderstands the principle of edge detection (detecting intensity changes in the image).\n\nExplains why normalization is needed before feeding images into a neural network.\n\nIf AI provides the code, the learner can rephrase and explain the method in their own words.\n\n3. AI-assisted Skill (20%)\n\nThe learner uses AI effectively as a tool rather than relying on it blindly.\n\nExamples:\n\nWrites a precise prompt such as “Generate Python code for computing homography using OpenCV with four pairs of matched points.”\n\nAfter AI provides code, the learner notices an error (e.g., wrong function arguments in cv2.findHomography).\n\nAdjusts AI code to run on their actual dataset (e.g., changing image paths, adapting for grayscale vs. color images, handling batch inputs).\n\nSimplifies redundant AI-generated code for better readability.\n\n4. Critical Thinking (10%)\n\nThe learner goes beyond execution and reflects on results.\n\nExamples:\n\nExplains why homography fails when points are nearly collinear.\n\nAnalyzes why edge detection produces too many false edges in noisy images.\n\nPoints out that AI-generated code works only for synthetic data but not for real-world images.\n\nSuggests simple improvements such as applying Gaussian smoothing before edge detection, or using RANSAC to improve homography robustness."
  ],
